      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the number of different settings for a suitcase lock with 3 dials, each of which can be a digit from 0 to 9, and all three digits must be different, we can use the concept of permutations.\n\nA permutation is an arrangement of all the members of a set into some sequence or order. Here, we need to find the number of ways to choose and arrange 3 digits out of 10, where the order matters.\n\nThe number of ways to choose and arrange 3 digits from 10 is given by the permutation formula \\( P(n, r) = \\frac{n!}{(n-r)!} \\), where \\( n \\) is the total number of items to choose from, \\( r \\) is the number of items to choose, and \\( ! \\) denotes factorial.\n\nIn this problem, \\( n = 10 \\) and \\( r = 3 \\). Therefore, we need to calculate \\( P(10, 3) \\):\n\n\\[\nP(10, 3) = \\frac{10!}{(10-3)!} = \\frac{10!}{7!}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify this by canceling out the \\( 7! \\) in the numerator and the denominator:\n\n\\[\nP(10, 3) = 10 \\times 9 \\times 8 \\times 7! \\div 7! = 10 \\times 9 \\times 8\n\\]\n\nNow, we perform the multiplication:\n\n\\[\n10 \\times 9 = 90\n\\]\n\\[\n90 \\times 8 = 720\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of different settings possible is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{720}\n\\]",
